WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:10.290
Hi, I'm Nicola and I want to
talk to you about translating

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:12.770
your Shiny apps into
multiple languages.

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:14.240
Some of you might
be disappointed

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:16.790
because when I say multiple
languages in Shiny apps,

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:18.890
talking about spoken
language, not

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:21.445
programming languages today.

00:00:21.445 --> 00:00:23.820
So, since I'm basically going
to spend a couple of minutes

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:25.950
telling you how annoying
it was to develop a Shiny

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:27.530
app in multiple
languages, I thought

00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:29.280
I'd start by telling
you why it's actually

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.980
a worthwhile thing to do.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:35.400
The vast majority of websites
and apps, not just Shiny apps,

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:37.590
are developed in English.

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:40.080
The problem with this is that
the vast majority of people

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:41.260
don't speak English.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:43.950
Only around two in 11 people
actually speak English,

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:45.840
and that's a large
number of people

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.720
to automatically exclude from
interacting with your Shiny

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:51.190
app.

00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:52.750
Language selectors
are becoming sort

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:55.013
of more commonplace in
traditional web development

00:00:55.013 --> 00:00:57.430
and we want to make sure that
Shiny apps don't sort of get

00:00:57.430 --> 00:00:58.035
left behind.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:04.140
Having said that,
translating Shiny

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.980
apps into multiple languages is
not that straightforward to do.

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:12.480
So what I want to
talk about today

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.600
is some of the small
issues that cropped up

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.410
when we were developing a
bilingual Shiny app to present

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:21.590
public health data.

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:24.990
Translation affects every single
component of your Shiny app.

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:28.670
Simple UI elements suddenly
became reactive expressions

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:31.440
that relied on a user input.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:33.390
There are a couple
of packages out there

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:35.970
that help you to automate
the translation process.

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:38.700
But what we found is that
those packages typically

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:41.267
only work for a few of the
most widely-spoken languages,

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:43.350
and that there are built
in dictionaries typically

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:46.200
didn't include domain
specific terms.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.510
So when we were developing
our bilingual app,

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:50.760
we built the translation
capabilities directly

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:53.010
into the app itself.

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:55.110
And during the
development process,

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:57.420
there were a couple of
seemingly small problems

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:00.150
that suddenly became
much bigger problems.

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:02.940
And one of those
problems was what

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:07.400
we thought was easy:
ordering a factor for a bar chart.

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:09.810
So most of you have
probably come across the fact

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:12.540
that R in the absence
of other instructions

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:15.600
will automatically sort
your character factors

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.580
and your factors alphabetically.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:19.360
However, this doesn't
always make sense.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:25.170
So, in this example, here we
have age groups encoded as character strings

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:27.300
and we might want to sort
them from young to old,

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:30.732
because that's easier for a
user to interpret.

00:02:30.732 --> 00:02:32.940
So for a single language,
this is really easy, right?

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:34.860
We just create a factor
and we explicitly

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:38.350
set the factor level.

00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:40.605
And we got a nicely
ordered bar chart.

00:02:40.605 --> 00:02:41.980
The problem is
that when we start

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:43.480
doing this with
multiple languages,

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:46.875
it is not quite so easy.

00:02:46.875 --> 00:02:49.250
First of all, we can't rely
on this alphabetical ordering

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:50.750
at all anymore.

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:52.730
If we sort of factor
in one language and we

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:53.930
sort it in another.

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:56.210
Actually, they're still
in different orders now.

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:59.500
So we can't rely on
alphabetical ordering at all.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:02.530
We also don't want to have
to manually specify factor

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:03.945
levels for all languages.

00:03:03.945 --> 00:03:05.320
It's a little bit
annoying if you

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:07.108
have a few words
in two languages,

00:03:07.108 --> 00:03:08.650
but if you have
hundreds or thousands

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:13.650
of words in multiple languages,
it is completely unmanageable.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:16.350
So, to solve this problem for
our app, what we did was we

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:20.010
ended up using the default or
primary language for everything

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:21.760
related to data processing.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:25.120
So in our case, we did
everything in English.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:29.710
And we explicitly set these
factor levels in English only.

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:30.713
For our bar chart example,

00:03:30.713 --> 00:03:32.380
what that meant was
essentially creating

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:34.420
three different functions.

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:38.050
One function to compute the
breakpoint for the x-axis.

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:41.500
Again, this is done in English
only based on our data set.

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:43.270
We then created
a second function

00:03:43.270 --> 00:03:45.790
to compute the labels based
on the output from that breaks

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:49.920
function and from the
user's choice of language.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:51.840
Essentially we
wanted to translate

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:55.420
as little as possible.

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:59.470
So I hope the last few
minutes have convinced you

00:03:59.470 --> 00:04:01.930
that translating Shiny apps, although
it's not straightforward,

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:05.170
is a very useful and
worthwhile thing to do.

00:04:05.170 --> 00:04:08.815
What we found our biggest lesson
was that when we were building,

00:04:08.815 --> 00:04:10.690
this translation was
always the first problem

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:12.970
that we actually had
to think about if we

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:14.180
wanted to add something new.

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:16.360
But to keep our app
actually efficient,

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:18.579
it was always the last code to run.

00:04:18.579 --> 00:04:20.850
So I hope this has been useful.

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:21.829
Find me on Twitter.

